// BASELINE — the schema as it stood when migrations were adopted.
//
// GENERATED, then frozen. Captured with SHOW CREATE TABLE from a database built
// by the db.sync({alter:true}) this replaces. With 75 tables, hand transcription
// is not a realistic option and a single wrong column type would be invisible on
// every existing database (the baseline skips tables that exist), surfacing only
// the first time somebody built a fresh environment.
//
// NEVER REGENERATE THIS FILE. A baseline describes the schema at ITS point in
// history; regenerating it later rewrites history to match whatever the models
// say then — the one thing a migration must not do.
//
// FOREIGN KEY CHECKS ARE DISABLED FOR THE CREATE PASS, and this is not laziness.
// 20 of these tables form a genuine CYCLE (bookings <-> vehicles, and others),
// so NO creation order exists that satisfies every foreign key — a topological
// sort of the 62 FK columns leaves 20 tables unorderable. mysqldump does exactly
// this for the same reason. The constraints are still DEFINED; only their
// enforcement is suspended while the empty tables are created, and re-enabled
// immediately after.
//
// Each table is created ONLY if absent, and the migration is recorded either
// way, so both cases end correct:
//   FRESH     no tables            -> all 75 created
//   EXISTING  built by alter-sync  -> nothing created, just recorded
//
// A NOTE ON WHAT THIS FREEZES. These statements come from a LOCALLY-synced
// database — i.e. the schema the MODELS describe. If a deployed database has
// drifted from the models (columns alter-sync dropped, or added by hand), this
// baseline does not know about it and, because it skips existing tables, will
// never notice. Run scripts/diffSchema.js against a restored copy of production
// BEFORE trusting this on that environment.
